The article is laid out in columns and scrolls sideways. Your wheel pushes it
left and right instead of up and down.

This page also loads `js  lens` — press **l** and a magnifier follows your
pointer, enlarging whatever paragraph sits under it. Forty lines of vanilla JS,
no dependencies, and it removes itself under reduced-motion.

## why bother

Because a page can be a room rather than a scroll, and because the constraint
of a fixed height forces a different kind of writing. Columns break where the
viewport says, not where you meant, and that argues with you.

## the js hook

Any block can set `js  name`, which loads `/js/name.js`. That is the whole
mechanism. Write a file, name it in a header, and the page has behaviour.
Nothing bundles, nothing compiles, nothing phones home.

## limits

A horizontal page is genuinely worse on a phone, and a lens is useless on a
touchscreen. Both are here anyway. Not everything has to work everywhere —
that is what [[backwards]] and this page are for, and why the rest of the site
is boring on purpose.
